  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 PM EDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\n- Isolated showers this evening\n\nSurface temperatures have warmed a bit and dew points have \nincreased a couple of notches as compared to the last couple of \ndays. This is evident in the satellite imagery with quite a bit \nmore cumulus development over the area today away from Lake \nMichigan. This is due to the increased flow ahead of the cold \nfront that is over Central Wisconsin as of just before 2 pm. \n\nEven though the sfc is seeing a bit of moisture ahead of the \nfront, the column as a whole is relatively dry with a lack of a \ngood feed of deep moisture present. In addition, the upper wave \nsupporting the front remains over Southern Ontario, with little to\nno deep forcing down this way. This continues the mainly dry \nforecast with about a 10% chance of something popping up with the \nfront late this afternoon and evening. \n\n- A few Showers Sunday afternoon\n\nThe first half of Sunday will be dry with cooler air coming in \nbehind the departing cold front. We will see another chance of \nrain develop Sunday afternoon, over mainly the southern portion of\nthe forecast area. \n\nThe feature responsible for this chance of rain is another upper \nlevel short wave that digs a bit further SE than the one \nresponsible for today's front. The forcing with this wave, along \nwith the convergence and limited moisture pooling along the sfc \nfront will provide for a band or two of showers Sunday afternoon \nwith diurnal heating. Limited low level moisture and temps will \nkeep thunder from being possible as sfc based and elevated LI's \nare well above zero C, and MU CAPE is zero. \n\nThis should move south of the area by 00z Monday, and allow sfc \nridging to build overhead for Sunday night and most of Monday. The\nair mass will be noticeable cooler behind the Sunday front. \n\n- Unsettled weather Tuesday and beyond with rain chances daily\n\nSSW flow at the sfc and aloft will increase quickly late Monday \nnight on the back side of the sfc ridge, and ahead of the next \nsystem. This system is currently an upper low just off of the \nPacific Northwest. This system will rotate eastward and eventually\nnortheast by Tuesday. \n\nThe warm front will move through Monday evening, with little to \nno precipitation for our area through the daylight hours, as the \nlow level jet and moisture axis stays well west of the area. The \nlow level jet moves overhead on Tuesday ahead of the front, but \nmuch of the showers/storms will stay north of the area. We will \nsee some rain chances on Tuesday, but much of the activity will \nstay NW.\n\nThe cold front is forecast to stall out, just as it passes south \nof the forecast area Tuesday night. It becomes parallel to the \nupper flow with the main energy driving NE into Canada.\n\nThe rest of the forecast period through next Saturday will be \nfocused on the stalled out front near the area. We will see short \nwave activity ride along the upper flow from the WSW, and initiate\nrounds of showers and storms. The exact placement of the front at\nany time is quite uncertain, and thus the rain chances will be \nalso. The front will be close enough through the entire period at \nthis time to justify chc pops right on through next Saturday.",
